From: Semih Baskan <strst.gs@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 13:42:38 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] ARM: dts: BCM5301X: switch USB VBUS with the USB3 PHY

A USB 3.0 stick that is plugged in at boot can come up on the EHCI at
USB 2.0 speed and stay there until it is replugged. On these Northstar
boards the USB VBUS switch is on from the bootloader, or is switched on
when bcma-hcd probes, well before the xHCI driver loads. The USB 2.0
lines of the USB 3.0 connector go to the separate EHCI, so a stick that
powers up with no SuperSpeed host falls back to USB 2.0 there, and some
sticks do not retry when the xHCI starts.

Make the regulator of the USB 3.0 port the phy-supply of the USB3 PHY,
without regulator-boot-on, and take it off the vbus-supply of the USB
cores. The rail is then off from when the regulator registers until the
xHCI driver powers the PHY, right before the controller starts. On the
R8000 the regulator node sits inside the pcie_bridge1 node, where
nothing creates a device for it, so every consumer of it defers for
good; it moves to the root node.

Boards with a switch already in the DT: R7000, R8000, WXR-1750DHP,
WZR-1166DHP and DHP2, WZR-1750DHP, EA6300 v1, EA6500 v2, R6250,
R6300 v2, RT-AC3200, WXR-1900DHP, EA9200, Archer C9 v1, RT-AC3100,
RT-AC88U, RT-AC5300, DIR-885L, DIR-890L, EA9500, ABR-4500, XBR-4500,
XWR-3100 and XWR-3150 v1. Where a board has a separate switch for its
USB 2.0 port, that one stays as it is.

Boards whose DT had no switch get one, taken from the vendor firmware:
RT-N18U (GPIO 13, active high), RT-AC56U (GPIO 9, active high; early
units have a second switch on GPIO 10, left as it is), RT-AC68U
(GPIO 9, active high), RT-AC87U (GPIO 9, active high) and WZR-900DHP
(GPIO 10, active low, from the stock kernel).

Where one GPIO feeds both ports (R7000, R8000, RT-N18U, RT-AC3200,
RT-AC3100, RT-AC88U, RT-AC5300, RT-AC56U, RT-AC68U, RT-AC87U,
WXR-1900DHP and WZR-900DHP), the USB 2.0 port gets power only once the
xHCI driver loads. Every boot power-cycles the USB devices on all of
these boards.

Tested on an RT-N18U with a SanDisk 3.2Gen1 stick, which stayed at
USB 2.0 after 3 of 3 power cycles without this change. With it, the
stick came up at SuperSpeed after 3 of 3 power cycles and 3 of 3
reboots, and a stick in the USB 2.0 port enumerated after a reboot. The
other boards are untested.

Signed-off-by: Semih Baskan <strst.gs@gmail.com>
